00:31:07,560 --> 00:31:11,579
He doesn’t know very much about Tom, though he says he’s read a Chicago paper

419
00:31:11,579 --> 00:31:18,000
for years just on the chance of catching a glimpse of Daisy’s name.” It was dark now,

420
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:22,079
and as we dipped under a little bridge I put my arm around Jordan’s golden

421
00:31:22,079 --> 00:31:26,880
shoulder and drew her toward me and asked her to dinner.

422
00:31:26,880 --> 00:31:32,779
Suddenly I wasn’t thinking of Daisy and Gatsby anymore but of this clean, hard, limited person,

423
00:31:32,779 --> 00:31:40,260
who dealt in universal skepticism and who leaned back jauntily just within the circle of my arm.

424
00:31:40,260 --> 00:31:45,060
A phrase began to beat in my ears with a sort of heady excitement:

425
00:31:45,060 --> 00:31:51,658
“There are only the pursued, the pursuing, the busy, and the tired.”

426
00:31:51,658 --> 00:31:56,520
“And Daisy ought to have something in her life,” murmured Jordan to me.

427
00:31:56,520 --> 00:32:00,859
“Does she want to see Gatsby?” “She’s not supposed to know about it.

428
00:32:00,859 --> 00:32:06,899
Gatsby doesn’t want her to know. You’re just supposed to invite her to tea.”

429
00:32:06,899 --> 00:32:11,819
We passed a barrier of dark trees, and then the facade of 59th Street, a block

430
00:32:11,819 --> 00:32:15,658
of delicate pale light, beamed down into the park.

431
00:32:15,658 --> 00:32:21,179
Unlike Gatsby and Tom Buchanan, I had no girl whose disembodied face floated

432
00:32:21,179 --> 00:32:26,158
along the dark cornices and blinding signs, and so I drew up the girl beside

433
00:32:26,158 --> 00:32:33,599
me, tightening my arms. Her wands scornful mouth smiled, and so I drew her up again closer,

434
00:32:33,599 --> 00:32:36,979
this time to my face
